---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---

<SiteLayout title="Method - Harbor Legal Independent Index" description="How Harbor Legal Independent Index counts bills, legal sources, and higher-court signals." activeNav="method">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
      <h1>How we count.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Harbor Legal Independent Index is methodology-first. We prefer fewer, cleaner counts to many noisy ones. Every
        report names the dataset, the jurisdiction, the legal source, and the collection lane.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <article class="surface">
          <div class="card-label">Bills</div>
          <div class="card-value">Source &amp; scope</div>
          <p class="card-copy">
            Bills are sourced from public legislative trackers. Each bill is tagged by topic,
            sponsor party, and stage reached (introduced, reported, passed chamber, signed).
            Reports state the chamber and window used.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="surface">
          <div class="card-label">Courts</div>
          <div class="card-value">Limited inclusion</div>
          <p class="card-copy">
            Court content is not the default product lane. If included, it is limited to federal
            circuit courts, state supreme courts, and the Supreme Court where the signal matters
            for nationwide or statewide legal trend analysis.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="surface">
          <div class="card-label">Monitoring</div>
          <div class="card-value">Windows and drift</div>
          <p class="card-copy">
            Trend comparisons use rolling windows, but source monitoring also tracks endpoint
            drift, schema changes, blocking behavior, and whether a jurisdiction is stable enough
            to trust in comparative views.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="surface">
          <div class="card-label">Publishing</div>
          <div class="card-value">Search first, reports second</div>
          <p class="card-copy">
            Search and source coverage come first. Reports package the most stable findings after
            the collection lane, source structure, and endpoint quality are understood.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="surface">
        <p class="eyebrow">Caveats</p>
        <h2 style="margin:0 0 0.6rem">Things we are explicit about</h2>
        <div class="signal-stack">
          <div class="signal-item">
            Bill counts include re-introductions; reports note this when material.
          </div>
          <div class="signal-item">
            Court coverage is intentionally narrow; lower-court case law is outside the main scope.
          </div>
          <div class="signal-item">
            State-level data lags federal; state-only reports name the source jurisdictions.
          </div>
          <div class="signal-item">
            No predictions. Harbor Legal Independent Index reports describe what happened, not what will happen.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
